From 1076a3598b65c8ad6d18b45892011ed136f62462 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 10:43:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 080/206] feat(shorebird): WrapCode, read out of the dSYM's
 inline line records

Four earlier attempts stopped here and the fourth wrote down why: the body
exists only inlined, 1588 bytes of undifferentiated machine code, and the two
out-parameters could not be told apart from ReadInstructions' locals. The
stated unblock conditions were a non-inlined copy or behavioural differencing.
Neither happened. A third way did.

Every callee inlined into WrapCode carries DW_AT_call_line, and line
attribution survives inlining by design -- that is what line tables are for.
Reading those records turns the 1588 bytes into a short list with an address
range each:

  :929 InstructionsLengthAt   :935 ASSERT(IsExecutable)  :936 return identity
  :941 IsLinkingEnabled       :942 ASSERT                :944/:945 wrap only
  :950 RunTargetForPatchIndex :951 WrapperForRunTarget   :952 Stats::Record
  :955 EntryPointForOffset    :958 AssertInstructionMatch
  :959 FindEntry              :967 rodata  :969 SetCodeAt  :970/:972 return

With the ranges known, the disassembly is targeted rather than blind. The
payoff is at 0x73b078: the EntryPoints returned on the linked path is built
from x23, which 0x73af24 computed as base_start_pc + offset. A patch entry
whose code matches the base resolves to the base image's address, and its Code
is registered in the base table -- which is what keeps pc-to-Code lookups on
unpatched addresses working. Note SetCodeAt appears twice, at :969 on the base
table and at :10225 on the patch's; they are not the same call.

AssertInstructionMatch also fell out: 0x73af28 loads one 32-bit word from each
address and compares them, failing an assert at :919. One word, not the body --
they pass no length.

Two upstream-class additions come with it, both named by the same records:
InstructionsTable::EntryPointForOffset (:955) and InstructionsLengthAt (:929).
The latter is the length computation ReadInstructions had inline, moved to
where theirs keeps it.

DELIBERATE DEVIATION, marked in the header: theirs takes uint32_t& and uword&
out-parameters and we do not. Neither dSYM names them, no artifact on this
machine has an out-of-line copy, and after optimisation the writes are
indistinguishable from the caller's own locals. Our caller computes what it
needs itself. Inventing a meaning would be the freelancing rule 3 forbids.

Built in the configuration that actually defines DART_PRECOMPILED_RUNTIME --
dartaotruntime, not run_vm_tests, which is trap 17 in this project's notes.

Negative control, because green after a refactor can just mean the new code is
not reached: returning payload_start + 4 on the non-linking path turns
dd_runtime_check red with 5 failures, so WrapCode is on the live path for all
1380 entry points. Reverted by the reverse patch. 102 tests pass and
verify_all.sh reports 5 ran, 0 failed, 0 skipped.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

